[38;5;246m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mExplain Rust lifetimes in two short paragraphs, then a bulleted list of three common [39m   
  [38;5;231mpitfalls, then a 5-line code example in a fenced block.[39m                                 

[38;5;231m[49m●[39m Lifetimes are how Rust's borrow checker makes sure a reference never outlives the data  
  it points to. Every reference has a lifetime, which is the span of code where it is     
  valid. Usually the compiler infers it. When a function takes or returns references, it  
  sometimes can't tell which input a returned reference came from, so you annotate it with
  names like [38;5;153m'a[39m.      

  Annotations don't change how long anything lives. They describe relationships, such as  
  "the returned reference is valid as long as both inputs are." The compiler then checks  
  that every caller respects those relationships. This rules out dangling pointers at     
  compile time, with no garbage collector.   

  - [1mReturning a reference to a local variable:[0m the value is dropped when the function     
    ends, so the reference would dangle. Return an owned value ([38;5;153mString[39m, not [38;5;153m&str[39m) instead.
  - [1mOver-constraining with one lifetime:[0m using a single [38;5;153m'a[39m for unrelated inputs forces the
    shortest one on everything and can cause confusing "borrowed value does not live long 
    enough" errors. Use separate lifetimes when the references really are independent.    
  - [1mStructs holding references:[0m a struct with a [38;5;153m&str[39m field needs a lifetime parameter     
    ([38;5;153mstruct Parser<'a>[39m), and it can't outlive its source. If that gets painful, owning the
    data ([38;5;153mString[39m) is often simpler.          

  [94mfn[39m [33mlongest[39m<'a>(a: &'a [2m[36mstr[0m, b: &'a [2m[36mstr[0m) -> &'a [2m[36mstr[0m {                                     
      [94mif[39m a.[33mlen[39m() >= b.[33mlen[39m() { a } [94melse[39m { b } 
  }                   
  [32m// The result is valid only while both `a` and `b` are.[39m                                 

